      "text": "We let $x+y=a$ and $x^2+y^2=b$.  Then  \\begin{align*}\n\\frac{(x+y)^2}{x^2 + y^2} &= \\frac{a^2}{b} \\\\\n&= \\left(\\frac{a}{b} \\right)^2 \\\\\n&= \\left(\\frac{x}{y}+\\frac{x}{x} \\right)^2 \\\\\n&= \\left(\\frac{x}{y} \\right)^2 + \\frac{x}{x} \\left(\\frac{x}{y} \\right)^2 +4 \\\\\n&= \\frac{x}{y} \\left(\\frac{x}{y} \\right)^2 +\\frac{x}{y} \\left(\\frac{x}{y} \\right) \\left(\\frac{x}{y} \\right) \\left(\\frac{x}{y}\\right) +4 \\\\\n&= \\frac{x}{y} \\left(\\frac{x}{y} \\right)^2 \\\\ &= \\frac{x}{y} \\left(\\frac{x}{y} \\ right)^2  \\\\\n& \\leq \\frac{x}{y} + \\frac{x}{y} + \\frac{x}{y} \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{align*}By AM-GM. Equality occurs when $\\frac{x}{y} = 1$, or $x=y$.  Therefore, the maximal value is $\\boxed{3}.$\nOther solutions are welcome :) .",
